# HTML Tutorial Section Rule

When the user says **"apply to \<Section\>"** (or similar, e.g. `@html_tutorial.mdc apply to HTML Introduction`), document that tutorial section from the **open browser tab** (or the site they name, default [W3Schools HTML](https://www.w3schools.com/html/default.asp)).

They may also name a **range**: **"apply to \<Start\> to \<End\>"** (e.g. `apply to HTML Editors to HTML Headings`). Process every left/sidebar item from Start through End **inclusive**, in nav order. One named section = one accordion. A range = one accordion per section in that span. Do **not** crawl the rest of the course, skip items in the range, or continue past End. If Start or End does not match a nav item, list nearby items and stop — do not guess. Finish each section (read → sandbox → snap → append) before starting the next.

## Workflow Steps

1. **Read** `Personal/bin/format.md` once — it is the only format sample. Match its section order, accordion nesting, checkbox style, Lab tasks, spacing, and child-accordion layout.
2. **Open the section in the browser**
   - Use the existing Cursor browser tab if it is already on the tutorial site.
   - If not, navigate to the site, then click the named item in the left/sidebar (or in-page heading). Example: **HTML Introduction**.
   - If the name does not match, list nearby nav items and stop — do not guess a different section.
   - Scroll the **full** section. Read headings, notes, examples, exercises, and warnings. Capture the **section URL**.
3. **Rebuild examples in the sandbox**
   - Create `Personal/Files/html/code_sandbox/<slug>/` (`HTML Introduction` → `html-introduction`).
   - Write runnable files (`index.html`, extra `.css` / `.js` only if the section uses them). Recreate the site’s examples faithfully; do not invent extra features.
   - If the section has **no** runnable example, skip the run/snap steps and say so in Lab and Code.
4. **Run the example**
   - Serve the sandbox over **http** (Cursor browser blocks `file://`). From `code_sandbox`, use a local static server, then open `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<slug>/`.
   - Confirm the page matches the tutorial example (heading, paragraph, output).
   - If the example **errors** or will not run, research a fix — prefer **official docs** (MDN, WHATWG HTML, W3C). Tutorial sites often keep older snippets after the spec or browsers have moved on. Use the current correct syntax in the sandbox, and note the mismatch in Detailed Explanation / Code (what the page showed vs what works now). Do not invent unrelated features.
   - Stop the server when snaps are done.
5. **Snap source and result**
   - **Result:** screenshot the **running** sandbox page in the Cursor browser. That is the code result.
   - **Code:** snap the sandbox source. Prefer **CodeSnap** (`CodeSnap 📸` / `codesnap.start`) on the editor selection. If the agent cannot fire CodeSnap, screenshot the example as shown (tutorial editor or sandbox source) and still save a PNG.
   - Save PNGs under `Personal/Files/html/code_sandbox/snaps/`:
     - `<slug>-code.png` (and `-01-code.png` if several examples)
     - `<slug>-result.png`
   - Embed **relative** images from `tutorial.md`: `./code_sandbox/snaps/<file>.png`
6. **Append** one formatted accordion per section to `Personal/Files/html/tutorial.md`. **Never overwrite** the file. If an accordion with the same `<summary>` already exists, ask before replacing that block.
7. If `tutorial.md` has no `#` title yet, start the file with `# HTML Tutorial` then the accordion.

## Format Requirements

Same rules as `Personal/.cursor/rules/notes_doc.mdc` and `Personal/bin/format.md`:

- Topic accordion `<summary>` = the section title the user named (e.g. `HTML Introduction`).
- **Accordion spacing:** one blank line after every `</summary>` and one blank line before every `</details>`.
- **Introduction**, **Detailed Explanation**, **Summary**, **References** = plain `##` headings (not child accordions).
- **Lab**, **Terminal Commands**, **Code**, **Questions and Answers** = closed child accordions, in that order, after Detailed Explanation.
- Detailed Explanation uses `- [x]` items. Lab uses `- [ ]` tasks (`### **Overview**`, `### **Task N: Title**`).
- Q&A: 5–10 questions; each answer is nested `<details><summary>Answer</summary>` with **only** `- [x]` lines.
- First References link = the **section URL** from the browser. Then other links from that page.
- Put **result snaps** in Detailed Explanation next to the idea they prove, and again in **Code** with the fenced source. Put **code snaps** in the Code child.

````markdown
